    "done_when": "the follow read loop in reckon/crew/runs.py never parses or advances past a final line that lacks its newline. It holds the partial bytes and completes the record on a later read, and the recorded stream offset only ever lands on a line boundary. reckon/cli.py _follow_boundary finds the last newline by reading backward from the captured size in bounded chunks of at most 64 KiB rather than reading the whole stream. Only _follow_boundary changes in reckon/cli.py. A new tests/test_follow_reads_whole_lines.py starts the real follow loop, writes half a record, lets the loop read, completes the record, and asserts it is delivered exactly once with its full content. A second case asserts _follow_boundary reads no more than a bounded number of bytes on a stream larger than 1 MB. The declared mutation, accepting a line without its newline, fails the first case with its log recorded including an EXIT= line. The follow, ticker, watch and row-policy files named in the after_suite command of r-20260929T093259536095-follow-boundary-snaps-to-a-line, plus the new file, show zero added failures by id against the dispatch base. Compare the noise-pair case over five repeated runs per arm and report both counts. baseline_suite and after_suite are one-line JSON with revision, tree, full command, exit_status, absolute log_path, completed, failure_count and failure_ids, each log headed with its revision and tree",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "the follower delivers every stream record once, whole, even when the producer is still writing it when the follow loop reads, by holding a partial final line until its newline arrives",
